#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "da92ef6c-cb0b-5a4c-9a0d-0f0a9db19616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4403" :question/problem "Let\n$[\\begin{aligned}19 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product $A\\times B$ is well defined\n\n- The product $B\\times A$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, and $B$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{2}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{2}} \\times 3}{B} = \n\t{\\color{green}\\text{well defined}}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $B$ and $2$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{red}{3}}{B} \\underset{\\color{red}\\neq}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{red}2} \\times 2}{A} = {\\color{red}\\text{undefined}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38167e2-3c1d-5c00-9691-ba8e6ac6fdce" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices Given the Dimensions of the Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "c677ee1a-d3c2-534c-985c-efaa5dc04c86" :question/math-academy-id "e-4404" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix. What are the dimensions of $AB$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication, and $AB$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0a4fa3d-fc1a-5e95-a632-f3a25c9bc4b1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "9352349d-64e0-5437-9d72-7e7f7e571064" :question/math-academy-id "e-4405" :question/problem "Let\n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2 \\times 4$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $BA$ has dimensions $2 \\times 2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{B} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{A} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{BA}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38167e2-3c1d-5c00-9691-ba8e6ac6fdce" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices Given the Dimensions of the Factors"}] :ques